---
import Gallery from '../../layouts/Gallery.astro';
import Related from '../../components/Related.astro';
import Demo from '../../components/Demo.astro';
import pkg from '@busy-office/ui/package.json';
import { createRequire } from 'node:module';
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs';
import { gzipSync } from 'node:zlib';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

/* Sizes computed from the shipped artifact at build, same as index.astro —
   the hand-typed "70 kB / 11 kB" beside the computed {pkg.version} had
   drifted to 75.9/12.5 before anyone noticed (2026-08-21 sweep). */
const require = createRequire(import.meta.url);
const minCss = readFileSync(require.resolve('@busy-office/ui/css/min'), 'utf8');
const minKb = (minCss.length / 1024).toFixed(0);
const gzipKb = (gzipSync(Buffer.from(minCss)).length / 1024).toFixed(1);

/* Windowed list — the scanning-scale behavior (roadmap 30.4b). Three
   chunks, offsets 0/50/100 of a 50,000-row total; the middle one is what a
   reader is looking at, so it's the one shown fully loaded — the other two
   illustrate the evicted-spacer shape rather than padding the page with
   150 real rows.

   SPACER HEIGHTS ARE ILLUSTRATIVE (roadmap 173.1). At their true size —
   50 rows x ~40px — each spacer is 2,000px, so the table ran 4,200px and
   the four loaded rows sat 2,000px below the header: a reader saw two
   screens of blank and never reached the thing being demonstrated. They
   are shown at 120px, and the copied markup says so on the line above
   each one, because `Demo` renders the preview and the copyable code from
   ONE string — a shortened height that did not admit it would be a 154.2
   (a sample that silently misleads the person pasting it). The real
   formula stays in the comment and in the caption below the demo. */
const windowedMarkup = `<table class="bo-data-table" data-windowed data-window-chunks="3"
       data-table-total-rows="50000">
  <thead><tr><th scope="col">Row</th><th scope="col">Reference</th></tr></thead>
  <!-- Scrolled past and evicted: ONE spacer stands in for this chunk's 50
       rows. Height = rowCount x a row height measured from one real row at
       the FIRST eviction and cached, so no eviction after that forces a
       layout read. Shown at 120px so the whole shape fits one screen;
       running, this spacer is about 2,000px. -->
  <tbody data-chunk-id="c0" data-chunk-offset="0" data-evicted="true">
    <tr class="bo-data-table__spacer" style="block-size: 120px"><td colspan="2"></td></tr>
  </tbody>
  <!-- The chunk currently in view: real rows, real data-row-id. -->
  <tbody data-chunk-id="c1" data-chunk-offset="50">
    <tr data-row-id="R-51"><td>51</td><td>REF-000051</td></tr>
    <tr data-row-id="R-52"><td>52</td><td>REF-000052</td></tr>
    <tr data-row-id="R-53"><td>53</td><td>REF-000053</td></tr>
    <tr data-row-id="R-54"><td>54</td><td>REF-000054</td></tr>
  </tbody>
  <!-- Not yet scrolled to: evicted the same way, symmetrically — this is
       bidirectional windowing, not append-only infinite scroll. Same
       illustrative 120px; same real ~2,000px. -->
  <tbody data-chunk-id="c2" data-chunk-offset="100" data-evicted="true">
    <tr class="bo-data-table__spacer" style="block-size: 120px"><td colspan="2"></td></tr>
  </tbody>
</table>`;

/* Token-mapped AG Grid theme — the recipe DESIGN.md promised ("a token-themed
   AG Grid recipe in the docs, never a grid engine of our own") and that no page
   had ever carried (roadmap 30.4a). Every custom property on the right is a
   real shipped token; the ones on the left are AG Grid's own theming API. */
const agGridRecipe = `/* your-app.css — after both stylesheets */
.ag-theme-quartz {
  --ag-background-color:            var(--bo-color-bg-surface);
  --ag-foreground-color:            var(--bo-color-text-primary);
  --ag-secondary-foreground-color:  var(--bo-color-text-secondary);
  --ag-header-background-color:     var(--bo-color-bg-surface-raised);
  --ag-border-color:                var(--bo-color-border-default);
  --ag-row-hover-color:             var(--bo-color-bg-hover);
  --ag-selected-row-background-color: var(--bo-color-accent-subtle);
  --ag-font-size:                   var(--bo-density-font-size);
  --ag-row-height:                  var(--bo-density-row-height);
  --ag-cell-horizontal-padding:     var(--bo-density-cell-padding-x);
}

/* Density comes along for free: the tokens above are already
   density-aware, so data-density="compact" on an ancestor
   re-sizes the grid with the rest of the screen. */`;
---
<Gallery title="Performance at scale">
  <p class="demo-note">
    Where this framework's numbers actually sit, measured rather than
    claimed, and the honest limits: what to do at 5,000 rows, 200
    columns, and on the ten-year-old laptop your users are issued.
  </p>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Measured, not guessed</h2>
    <p>Full table stack (sticky header, row checkboxes, badges, container
    query, <code>initDataTables()</code>) in the
    <code>examples/po-app</code> stress harness — the same numbers on the
    <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>data table page</a>, repeated
    here because they are the framework's scaling story:</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Rows</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Initial render</th><th scope="col" class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">Select-all</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">1,000</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">85 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">4 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">5,000</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">174 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">18 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">20,000</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">558 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">49 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">5,000 <span class="bo-badge">4× CPU throttle</span></td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">1,625 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">15 ms</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">20,000 <span class="bo-badge">4× CPU throttle</span></td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">3,783 ms</td><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">60 ms</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">The throttled rows approximate the
    corporate hardware ERP users actually get. Read them as the real
    numbers, not the optimistic ones.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The decisions those numbers imply</h2>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Situation</th><th scope="col">Do this</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Up to ~1,000 rows</td><td>Render them. Native scrolling, find-in-page and print all keep working.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>~1,000–5,000 rows</td><td>Still fine on modern hardware; add <a href={base + '/components/pagination'}>pagination or load-more</a> before it becomes a habit.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Beyond ~5,000, searching</td><td>Paginate server-side. The framework ships <strong>no virtualiser for search</strong> — see below.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Beyond ~5,000, scanning</td><td><a href={base + '/components/pagination'}>Load-more</a> while the feed is bounded; a <a href="#windowed-list">windowed list</a> only once memory must stay flat.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>200 columns</td><td>Horizontal scroll plus a frozen first column (<code>--sticky-col</code>); offer a column chooser rather than shrinking type past legibility.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bulk select at scale</td><td><code>initDataTables()</code> keeps the selection count O(1) rather than re-counting the DOM; the style flush after checking 20,000 boxes is the cost, and it is the browser's, not ours.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Slow aggregates on a dashboard</td><td>Load each widget as its own fragment so one slow query never blocks the screen — the <a href={base + '/patterns/reporting-dashboard'}>dashboard pattern</a>'s data contract.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>Why there is no virtualiser for search</h2>
    <p>Virtualisation buys smooth scrolling at 50,000 rows and costs
    find-in-page, native keyboard scrolling, correct print output, and
    screen-reader row counts — the things back-office users rely on daily.
    It also forces every consumer into a JS render loop, which is the
    opposite of this project's stance. Server-side pagination keeps all
    of it and is what a real ERP <em>search</em> screen does anyway — if
    your screen is genuinely about searching 50,000 rows, this framework's
    answer is still filters and page numbers, full stop.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>What changed</strong>: this refusal
    was narrowed, not reversed, on 2026-08-22 (roadmap 30.4b) — see below
    for the case it no longer covers.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo" id="windowed-list">
    <h2>Windowed list — the scanning exception</h2>
    <p><code>initWindowedList()</code> exists for the other case in the
    decision table above: <strong>scanning</strong>, not searching — a
    monitoring feed, an audit log, a list someone pages through start to
    finish rather than filters down. Everything above about search still
    holds; this doesn't reopen it, it names the one case it never covered.</p>
    <Demo code={windowedMarkup} />
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">A chunk that scrolls far enough away
    evicts to a <strong>computed-height spacer</strong>: its height is
    <em>rows &times; row height</em>, and that row height is measured from
    one real row <strong>once, at the first eviction</strong>, then cached —
    so no eviction after that forces a layout read on the exact operation
    this behavior exists to make cheap. (Deriving it from
    <code>--bo-density-row-height</code> instead was tried and reverted:
    real rows render taller than the token, so token-derived spacers ran
    ~250px short per 100-row chunk and the list jumped as you scrolled. The
    token remains the fallback when no real row has rendered yet.) A spacer
    re-requests its rows (the same <code>bo:table-load-more</code>
    event <a href={base + '/components/pagination'}>load-more</a> already
    fires, now carrying an optional chunk-offset detail) when scrolled
    back toward. Selection survives eviction too: a checked row's
    <code>data-row-id</code> lives in a Set outside the DOM, so a bulk
    action still reaches every row you picked, not just whatever's
    currently rendered.</p>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted"><strong>Named costs, not hidden ones</strong>:
    browser find-in-page cannot find an evicted row, and printing gets
    only what's currently loaded. Both are the same argument for
    filtering server-side first — windowing is for the real remainder
    after that, not a replacement for it. And it does not compose with
    <a href={base + '/reference/keyboard'}><code>data-grid-nav</code></a>'s
    two-axis keyboard grid in this version: that behavior's own
    <code>aria-rowindex</code> assumes DOM position equals true position,
    which windowing breaks by design.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>So what do I use, and when?</h2>
    <p>The previous section says what this framework will not build. This one
    says what to do instead, which is the half that was missing.</p>
    <div class="bo-data-table-container" tabindex="0">
      <table class="bo-data-table" data-density="compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Rows the user faces</th><th scope="col">What the user is doing</th><th scope="col">Use</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">up to ~2,000</td><td>reading and acting on a filtered list</td><td>plain <a href={base + '/components/data-table'}>data table</a> — nothing special</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">~2,000 – 50,000</td><td><strong>searching</strong>, then acting on a few</td><td><strong>filter server-side and paginate</strong> — <a href={base + '/components/pagination'}>page numbers</a>. The right answer for most ERP lists</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">~2,000 – 50,000</td><td>scanning forward through a bounded feed</td><td><a href={base + '/components/pagination'}>load-more</a> (<code>initLoadMore()</code>, with <code>data-load-more-auto</code> for infinite scroll) — memory grows as you scroll, fine for a feed nobody scrolls indefinitely</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">50,000+, unbounded</td><td>scanning where memory must stay flat, not just grow slower</td><td>a <a href="#windowed-list">windowed list</a> (<code>initWindowedList()</code>) — evicts what's off-screen instead of only ever appending</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="bo-data-table__col--numeric">any</td><td>tabbing across cells all day — planning boards, price matrices, allocation grids</td><td>a real grid. Roughly one screen in twenty; see the recipe below</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      The search and scanning rows can carry the same row count and still get
      different answers, because the question that decides it is not "how many
      rows" but <strong>what the user is doing with them</strong>. Someone
      searching wants filters and page numbers; someone scanning wants the next
      batch to appear — reaching for windowing when the real problem is a
      missing filter is still the most common way to make a screen slower and
      less accessible at once. Windowing earns its place only once scanning
      itself needs memory to stay flat, not merely once row counts get large.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The residual case: a real grid, themed with these tokens</h2>
    <p>For the one screen in twenty that genuinely is a spreadsheet, use a grid
    that already solved virtual scroll, and make it look like the rest of your
    app by pointing its variables at these tokens. AG Grid is the example
    because its theming is CSS variables; the same mapping idea works for any
    grid that exposes them.</p>
    <pre><code>{agGridRecipe}</code></pre>
    <p class="bo-u-text-muted">
      <strong>What you are buying and paying.</strong> You get virtual scroll, column
      virtualisation and cell editing that this framework deliberately does not own. You
      pay a JS render loop, a second theming system to keep in sync, and the
      accessibility work that windowing always costs — find-in-page misses unrendered
      rows, printing gets only what is rendered, and the grid must publish
      <code>aria-rowcount</code> itself or a screen reader reports the wrong size. Those
      are not reasons to avoid it on the screen that needs it; they are reasons not to
      reach for it on the nineteen that do not.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="demo">
    <h2>The bytes</h2>
    <p>The whole framework is <strong>{pkg.version}</strong> at {minKb} kB
    minified / {gzipKb} kB gzipped of CSS, plus optional behaviors — smaller
    than most single component libraries' JS runtime, and the CSS is
    cacheable across every screen. Per-component files ship too, so a
    single-screen pilot can import three files and nothing else. There is
    no build step to tune, no tree-shaking to configure, and no runtime
    cost per component instance: a table with 20,000 rows loads exactly
    the same stylesheet as a login form.</p>
  </section>

  <Related links={[
    ['/components/data-table', 'Data table'],
    ['/components/pagination', 'Pagination'],
    ['/patterns/list-report', 'List report'],
    ['/getting-started/scope', "What's in scope"],
  ]} />
</Gallery>
